# Conventional Commits Guide

## Overview

This project follows [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/en/v1.0.0/) specification for commit messages. All commits must follow this format to maintain a clear and structured history.

## Commit Message Format

### Basic Structure

```
<type>[optional scope]: <emoji> <description>

[optional body]

[optional footer(s)]
```

### Type and Emoji Reference

| Type     | Emoji | Code                    | Description                                    |
| :------- | :---- | :---------------------- | :--------------------------------------------- |
| feat     | ✨    | `:sparkles:`            | New feature or functionality                   |
| fix      | 🐛    | `:bug:`                 | Bug fix                                        |
| docs     | 📚    | `:books:`               | Documentation changes only                     |
| style    | 💎    | `:gem:`                 | Code style changes (formatting, whitespace)    |
| refactor | 🔨    | `:hammer:`              | Code refactoring without feature/fix           |
| perf     | 🚀    | `:rocket:`              | Performance improvements                       |
| test     | 🚨    | `:rotating_light:`      | Adding or updating tests                       |
| build    | 📦    | `:package:`             | Build system or dependencies changes           |
| ci       | 👷    | `:construction_worker:` | CI/CD configuration changes                    |
| chore    | 🔧    | `:wrench:`              | Other changes that don't modify src/test files |
| revert   | ⏪    | `:rewind:`              | Revert a previous commit                       |

## Examples from This Project

### Feature Additions

```
feat: ✨ complete content replacement with professional AI consulting content (#9)
feat: ✨ add performance monitoring workflow and improve actions robustness (#8)
```

### Bug Fixes

```
fix: 🐛 correct build time output in performance analysis step (#16)
```

### Chores

```
chore: 🔧 improve workflows, formatting, and documentation consistency (#15)
chore: 🔧 remove deprecated dependency audit job and streamline security workflow (#14)
chore: 🔧 update dependencies in package.json (#12)
```

### Documentation

```
docs: 📚 update contributing guidelines and add conventional commits table (#13)
```

## Breaking Changes

### Format

Include an exclamation mark after the type/scope and add a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer:

```
feat!: drop legacy auth

BREAKING CHANGE: removes basic auth in favor of OAuth2
```

Or with scope:

```
feat(api)!: remove deprecated endpoints

BREAKING CHANGE: /api/v1/old-endpoint has been removed, use /api/v2/endpoint instead
```

## Best Practices

### Do's ✅

- Use lowercase for type and description
- Include the appropriate emoji after the colon
- Keep the description concise (50 characters or less)
- Use imperative mood ("add" not "added" or "adds")
- Reference issue/PR numbers when applicable (e.g., `(#123)`)
- Add body text for complex changes
- Use scope when the change affects a specific part of the codebase

### Don'ts ❌

- Don't use past tense ("added", "fixed")
- Don't end the description with a period
- Don't omit the emoji
- Don't use vague descriptions ("update stuff", "fix things")
- Don't mix multiple unrelated changes in one commit

## Scopes (Optional but Recommended)

Common scopes in this project:

- `api` - API routes and endpoints
- `ui` - UI components and styling
- `docs` - Documentation files
- `deps` - Dependencies updates
- `config` - Configuration files
- `ci` - CI/CD workflows
- `seo` - SEO-related changes
- `a11y` - Accessibility improvements

### Examples with Scopes

```
feat(api): ✨ add contact form validation endpoint
fix(ui): 🐛 resolve theme toggle not persisting
docs(readme): 📚 update installation instructions
chore(deps): 🔧 upgrade Next.js to v15.3.4
```

## Commit Body Guidelines

Use the body to explain:

- **What** changed
- **Why** the change was necessary
- **How** it solves the problem

```
feat(auth): ✨ implement OAuth2 authentication

Replace basic authentication with OAuth2 to improve security
and enable social login integrations. This change includes:
- OAuth2 provider configuration
- Social login buttons (Google, GitHub)
- Updated user session management

Closes #45
```

## Footers

Common footer types:

- `Closes #123` - Closes an issue
- `Fixes #123` - Fixes an issue
- `Refs #123` - References an issue
- `BREAKING CHANGE:` - Describes breaking changes
- `Co-authored-by:` - Credits co-authors

## Quick Reference

### Simple Commit

```bash
git commit -m "feat: ✨ add dark mode toggle"
```

### With Body and Footer

```bash
git commit -m "fix: 🐛 resolve navigation menu overflow

The mobile menu was overflowing on small screens.
Updated responsive breakpoints and added scroll behavior.

Fixes #78"
```

### Breaking Change

```bash
git commit -m "feat!: ✨ migrate to TypeScript 5.0

BREAKING CHANGE: requires Node.js 18+ for TypeScript 5.0 support"
```

## Tools and Automation

### Commitlint (If Added)

The project can optionally use commitlint to enforce these rules automatically. Configuration would be in `.commitlintrc.json`.

### Git Hooks (If Added)

Use Husky to run commitlint on commit messages automatically.

## Validation Checklist

Before committing, verify:

- [ ] Correct type chosen
- [ ] Appropriate emoji included
- [ ] Description is clear and concise
- [ ] Using imperative mood
- [ ] Scope added if applicable
- [ ] Issue/PR referenced if applicable
- [ ] Breaking changes clearly marked
- [ ] No trailing period in description

